A||||9||fieroburton||changing oil ?||||5||
Z||000000||fieroburton||09-06-2001||03:12 PM||||what is the best way to change the oil on a 84 2m4 ?  what is the best oil to use ? (i heard mobile 1 is the best) never changed the oil on a fiero before<BR>so any tips will be helpfull  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR>thanx||||reg||5
Z||000001||OcalaFiero||09-06-2001||03:21 PM||||I usually just put the back end up on jack stands, (after driving the car for a while to warm the oil and loosen everything up). Drain the oil by removing the drain plug (silver nut on bottom of oil pan). Then all ow all the oil to drain out and then replace the drain plug. Then mover your drain pan undr the area of the oil filter and rmove it by forst loosening it with a filter wrench. I usually lower the car at this point to allow any oil around the filter pipe to drain out. Lube the new filter gasket with some of the drained oil and screw it into place. When you feel the filter contact with the block, give it another 1/4 to 1/2 turn. (Do not over tighten). Fill the oil and start the car, watch the oil pressure gauge and check for leaks. I use Castrol 10w40 in my 2.5, but it's always pretty warm here and it won't thin out as fast as some of the 30 weights will.<P>Hope this helps<P>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||09-06-2001||03:46 PM||||I pull the coil wire when I get ready to restart after an oil and filter change. Turn the motor over until I see oil pressure, then replace the coil wire. Probably unnecessary but I do it anyway. I've noticed it some times takes a few seconds to load the filter.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroFanaticus2||09-06-2001||03:49 PM||||I change it the same as I do any other car.<BR>I use penzoil 5w30 and penzoil filter.  I also change the air filter at the same time.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tigger||09-06-2001||03:55 PM||||I just changed mine this week.  Use the PF-52 instead of the shorter PF-47 filter (PF is for ACDelco filters, for other brands use the equivalent).  I use regular Valvoline 10W-30.<P>If you can, hold a paper towel or rag below the filter when you loosen it.  If you don't the oil runs right down into the engine mount, dang GM engineers.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 09-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wolfhound||09-06-2001||04:03 PM||||What Tigger said and your arm.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnF||09-06-2001||05:57 PM||||In my sweet runnin' '88, 120K+, 2.5L; every 3K (I don't like factory recommended change schedule), changed by a GM dealer (29 minutes or the next is FREE & mine usually is!).  <P>As factory ordered, 5W - 30W oil with PF-52 filter (permanent air filter by K & N).<P>The only real problem is that I have to tell the change people "the engine ain't in the front!"||||reg||14
Z||000007||rogergarrison||09-06-2001||06:40 PM||||too many horror stories of oil change businesses. Only takes a few minutes, and not that messy if your carefull. Even Lisa changes her own filter and oil.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||09-06-2001||06:59 PM||||I use Mobile 1....<P>The long filter is required as part of the recall compliance for 84 cars. It's optional on others. The original ACDelco PF51 has been replaced by PF52. <P>In the Fram number system the long filter will be xxx3980. xxx varies depending on what "grade" the filter is and who it's sold by. Allied Signal makes filters for other brands besides Fram. I think I remember Castrol is one of them.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jake_Dragon||09-07-2001||07:31 AM||||Make sure you check your old filter for it's gasket, it could get stuck on the block.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> ||||reg||1
